FUSION (work around the pattern): time ratios, per run then median [smallest-largest]; >1 means the fused form is faster
  READ WITH: the fused int8 / fp8 variants run the library's quantised schedule (the raw quantised bytes are copied with cp.async into shared memory and dequantised there, shared memory to shared memory, inside the kernel); the unfused variants dequantise in a separate kernel that writes bf16 to global memory and then run the bf16 cp.async schedule. The two attention kernels differ in schedule details and tile sizes (block_q, block_kv columns of the CSV), so a prologue pair is not a pure fusion effect; the epilogue pair runs the same kernel and schedule: only the epilogue differs.
  dequant_flash / dequant_compiled_flash / epilogue_flash are compositions of off-the-shelf pieces that a user has today, not a hand-fused quantised-cache attention kernel and not the best that could be written.
